<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Sylvester II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 946–1003</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Gerbert of Aurillac, Sylvester II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Sylvester II, born Gerbert of Aurillac, was a French scholar who became the first French pope, reigning from 999 to 1003. As a young monk he studied in Catalonia, at Vic and probably Ripoll, where he learned mathematics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> drawn from Arabic sources, and he later taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. He promoted the abacus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu-Arabic numerals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and worked closely with Emperor Otto III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Sylvester II" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister der Reichenauer Schule · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meister_der_Reichenauer_Schule_002_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
